WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.243
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:01.293 --> 00:00:01.889
I am Dr.

3
00:00:01.939 --> 00:00:08.435
Cesar Marolla, Instructor at Harvard University Division of Continuing Education.

4
00:00:08.485 --> 00:00:14.495
R M T I turns what we can measure into one score, and follows it over time.

5
00:00:14.545 --> 00:00:15.950
Here: lung cancer.

6
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.518
Case study: Sabakhtarishvili and colleagues, J T O Clinical and Research Reports, 2025.

7
00:00:21.568 --> 00:00:31.950
In three thousand six hundred forty-seven screened people, stage four at diagnosis was thirteen percent, versus thirty-five to forty-four percent under usual care.

8
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.950
Stage one. A small tumor, confined to the lung, with no lymph node spread.

9
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.950
Stage two. A larger tumor, or nearby lymph nodes are involved.

10
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.950
Stage three. Spread to central chest lymph nodes or neighboring structures.

11
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:59.950
Stage four. Distant metastasis, for example to the brain or bone.

12
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:00.945
Data assessing.

13
00:01:00.995 --> 00:01:03.068
Smoking burden informs exposure.

14
00:01:03.118 --> 00:01:06.651
The two-year cancer detection rate informs likelihood.

15
00:01:06.701 --> 00:01:12.026
And screening delivery, which lowers late-stage presentation, informs resilience.

16
00:01:12.076 --> 00:01:13.950
This mapping is illustrative.

17
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.012
R equals L times E times V times one minus rho.

18
00:01:18.062 --> 00:01:24.580
Here R is zero point zero nine eight, band two of the fixed five-band scale.

19
00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.950
Expected Annual Loss adds consequences.

20
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:28.772
Trajectory.

21
00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:32.879
Each review cycle updates vulnerability and resilience.

22
00:01:32.929 --> 00:01:43.411
In this synthetic example, as screening improves, R falls from zero point zero nine eight to zero point zero four four, band two to band one.

23
00:01:43.461 --> 00:01:45.950
An illustration, not a prediction.

24
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.605
What R M T I does.

25
00:01:47.655 --> 00:01:58.180
It quantifies risk as one score, assesses it on fixed bands, and tracks vulnerability and resilience across cycles.

26
00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:01.950
Treatment decisions stay with clinicians.

27
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.236
R M T I is a research prototype: uncalibrated, synthetic examples, not a medical device.

28
00:02:08.286 --> 00:02:12.307
Does trajectory add information beyond the current level?

29
00:02:12.357 --> 00:02:13.950
Thank you for watching.
